Pre-test question 1 (Variables, easy): Fill in the blank to correctly declare an integer variable named age:
Code template: {BLANK} age = 25;
Correct code: int age = 25;
Options: int | Int | integer | void
Correct option: int
Explanation: Java uses lowercase 'int' as the primitive integer type keyword.

Pre-test question 2 (Variables, easy): Fill in the blank with a valid Java variable name:
Code template: int {BLANK} = 10;
Correct code: int _count = 10;
Options: _count | 2count | my-var | class
Correct option: _count
Explanation: _count is valid. Variable names cannot start with a digit, contain hyphens, or use reserved keywords like 'class'.

Pre-test question 3 (Variables, medium): Fill in the blank to declare a variable that stores true or false:
Code template: {BLANK} isActive = true;
Correct code: boolean isActive = true;
Options: boolean | bool | Boolean | bit
Correct option: boolean
Explanation: Java's primitive boolean type stores true/false. 'Boolean' (uppercase) is the wrapper class, and 'bool' isn't a Java keyword.

Pre-test question 4 (Variables, medium): Fill in the blank so the condition correctly checks if score equals 10 (rather than assigning it):
Code template: int score = 10; if (score {BLANK} 10) { System.out.println("Match"); }
Correct code: int score = 10; if (score == 10) { System.out.println("Match"); }
Options: == | = | === | equals
Correct option: ==
Explanation: == compares two values. A single = is assignment, not comparison, and isn't valid as a boolean condition here.

Pre-test question 5 (Variables, hard): Fill in the blank so that b ends up 10 while a becomes 11 afterward:
Code template: int a = 10; int b = {BLANK}; System.out.println(a + " " + b);
Correct code: int a = 10; int b = a++; System.out.println(a + " " + b);
Options: a++ | ++a | a + 1 | a
Correct option: a++
Explanation: a++ is post-increment: b receives the current value of a (10) first, then a becomes 11.

Pre-test question 11 (Loops, easy): Fill in the blank so this loop counts i from 0 up to 9:
Code template: for (int i = 0; {BLANK} < 10; i++) { System.out.println(i); }
Correct code: for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) { System.out.println(i); }
Options: i | j | 10 | n
Correct option: i
Explanation: The loop condition must test the same variable (i) that was declared and is being incremented.

Pre-test question 12 (Loops, easy): Fill in the blank to create a loop that always runs its body at least once:
Code template: int i = 0; {BLANK} { System.out.println("run"); } while (i < 0);
Correct code: int i = 0; do { System.out.println("run"); } while (i < 0);
Options: do | while | for | if
Correct option: do
Explanation: do-while checks its condition after executing the body, so it always runs at least once.

Pre-test question 13 (Loops, medium): Fill in the blank so this loop terminates after printing 0 1 2:
Code template: int i = 0; while (i < 3) { System.out.print(i + " "); {BLANK}; }
Correct code: int i = 0; while (i < 3) { System.out.print(i + " "); i++; }
Options: i++ | i-- | i | continue
Correct option: i++
Explanation: Without incrementing i, the loop condition (i < 3) never becomes false — i++ is required to make progress.

Pre-test question 14 (Loops, medium): Fill in the blank so the loop stops immediately once i equals 3:
Code template: for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) { if (i == 3) { {BLANK}; } System.out.print(i); }
Correct code: for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) { if (i == 3) { break; } System.out.print(i); }
Options: break | sleep | wait | pause
Correct option: break
Explanation: break exits the nearest enclosing loop immediately.

Pre-test question 15 (Loops, hard): Fill in the blank so the output is "0 2 " (i = 1 is skipped, not printed):
Code template: for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) { if (i == 1) { {BLANK}; } System.out.print(i + " "); }
Correct code: for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) { if (i == 1) { continue; } System.out.print(i + " "); }
Options: continue | break | return | exit
Correct option: continue
Explanation: continue skips only the rest of the current iteration (the print), unlike break which would exit the loop entirely.

Pre-test question 16 (Arrays, easy): Fill in the blank to correctly declare an integer array:
Code template: int{BLANK} arr = new int[5];
Correct code: int[] arr = new int[5];
Options: [] | () | {} | <>
Correct option: []
Explanation: Square brackets after the type declare an array: int[] arr = new int[5];

Pre-test question 17 (Arrays, easy): Fill in the blank to correctly access the FIRST element of the array:
Code template: int[] arr = {10, 20, 30}; System.out.println(arr[{BLANK}]);
Correct code: int[] arr = {10, 20, 30}; System.out.println(arr[0]);
Options: 0 | 1 | -1 | arr.length
Correct option: 0
Explanation: Java arrays are zero-indexed — the first element is always at index 0.

Pre-test question 18 (Arrays, medium): Fill in the blank so this loop prints every element without going out of bounds:
Code template: int[] arr = new int[5]; for (int i = 0; i {BLANK} arr.length; i++) { System.out.println(arr[i]); }
Correct code: int[] arr = new int[5]; for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) { System.out.println(arr[i]); }
Options: < | <= | > | ==
Correct option: <
Explanation: Valid indices run from 0 to arr.length - 1. Using <= would read one index past the end of the array.

Pre-test question 19 (Arrays, medium): Fill in the blank to correctly get the number of elements the array can hold:
Code template: int[] arr = new int[7]; System.out.println(arr.{BLANK});
Correct code: int[] arr = new int[7]; System.out.println(arr.length);
Options: length | length() | size | count
Correct option: length
Explanation: Arrays expose length as a property (no parentheses) — unlike String's length() method.

Pre-test question 20 (Arrays, hard): Fill in the blank so sum correctly accumulates the total of all array elements:
Code template: int[] nums = {1,2,3,4,5}; int sum = 0; for (int n : nums) { sum {BLANK} n; } System.out.println(sum);
Correct code: int[] nums = {1,2,3,4,5}; int sum = 0; for (int n : nums) { sum += n; } System.out.println(sum);
Options: += | = | *= | -=
Correct option: +=
Explanation: += adds each element into the running total. Using = would overwrite sum with just the last element instead of accumulating.

Pre-test question 21 (Methods, easy): Fill in the blank to send a value back from this method:
Code template: static int getFive() { {BLANK} 5; }
Correct code: static int getFive() { return 5; }
Options: return | print | output | give
Correct option: return
Explanation: The return keyword exits the method and passes a value back to the caller.

Pre-test question 22 (Methods, easy): Fill in the blank so this method compiles correctly (it never returns a value):
Code template: static {BLANK} printHello() { System.out.println("Hello"); }
Correct code: static void printHello() { System.out.println("Hello"); }
Options: void | int | return | null
Correct option: void
Explanation: void means the method does not return any value.

Pre-test question 23 (Methods, medium): Fill in the blank so add(3, {BLANK}) prints 7:
Code template: static int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; } System.out.println(add(3, {BLANK}));
Correct code: static int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; } System.out.println(add(3, 4));
Options: 4 | four | "4" | IV
Correct option: 4
Explanation: add() expects two int arguments. 3 + 4 = 7 — the other options aren't valid int literals.

Pre-test question 24 (Methods, medium): Fill in the blank so the recursion has a base case that stops it:
Code template: static int mystery(int n) { if (n == 1) { return {BLANK}; } return n + mystery(n - 1); }
Correct code: static int mystery(int n) { if (n == 1) { return 1; } return n + mystery(n - 1); }
Options: 1 | 0 | n | mystery(n)
Correct option: 1
Explanation: The base case must return a fixed value (1) rather than recursing further, or the recursion never stops.

Pre-test question 25 (Methods, hard): Fill in the blank so each recursive call moves toward the base case (n == 1):
Code template: static int mystery(int n) { if (n == 1) { return 1; } return n + mystery({BLANK}); }
Correct code: static int mystery(int n) { if (n == 1) { return 1; } return n + mystery(n - 1); }
Options: n - 1 | n | n + 1 | 1
Correct option: n - 1
Explanation: Each call must decrease n so it eventually reaches the base case. Calling mystery(n) again would recurse forever.
